    {
      "type": "stat",
      "title": "Worst VM CPU ready",
      "description": "**CPU ready** is the share of time a VM's vCPUs were ready to run but had to wait for a physical core.\nVME reports it per VM as the average across its vCPUs (for a host: across the VMs on it).\n\n| Per vCPU | What it means | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **under 5%** | Healthy | Nothing |\n| **5 to 10%** | Contention: VMs are queuing for CPU | Check the host's CPU usage and vCPU:core ratio; watch the trend |\n| **over 10%** | VMs are noticeably slowed | Move VMs to a quieter host, cut vCPUs on oversized VMs, or add capacity |\n\nLook at sustained values: short spikes during backups, patching or boot storms are normal.\nToo many vCPUs per physical core raises ready time for every VM on the host, so right-size large VMs first.\n\nVME added this \"CPU Ready-equivalent metric\" in 9.1.0; HPE doesn't document how it's calculated or a threshold. At low load it reads several times higher than the kernel's\nown measurement (*vCPU wait* on the exporter dashboards), so these thresholds err on the side of warning early.\nWhen the two disagree, trust vCPU wait.\n\nThis tile: the highest per-VM average right now.",
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "grafana-postgresql-datasource",
            "uid": "vme-tsdb"
          },
          "refId": "A",
          "rawQuery": true,
          "editorMode": "code",
          "format": "table",
          "rawSql": "SELECT max(v) FROM (SELECT last(m.value, m.time) AS v FROM metrics m JOIN servers s ON s.id = m.entity_id WHERE m.time > now() - INTERVAL '15 minutes' AND m.entity_type = 'vm' AND m.metric = 'cpu_ready_pct' AND (-1 IN ($cluster) OR s.cluster_id IN ($cluster)) AND (-1 IN ($host) OR s.host_id IN ($host)) GROUP BY m.entity_id) x"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "percent",
          "color": {
            "mode": "thresholds"
          },
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "green",
                "value": null
              },
              {
                "color": "#EAB839",
                "value": 5
              },
              {
                "color": "red",
                "value": 10
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "colorMode": "value",
        "graphMode": "none",
        "textMode": "value",
        "justifyMode": "center"
      },
      "id": 9,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 4,
        "w": 6,
        "h": 4
      },
      "datasource": {
        "type": "grafana-postgresql-datasource",
        "uid": "vme-tsdb"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "stat",
      "title": "Worst single vCPU ready",
      "description": "**CPU ready** is the share of time a VM's vCPUs were ready to run but had to wait for a physical core.\nVME reports it per VM as the average across its vCPUs (for a host: across the VMs on it).\n\n| Per vCPU | What it means | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **under 5%** | Healthy | Nothing |\n| **5 to 10%** | Contention: VMs are queuing for CPU | Check the host's CPU usage and vCPU:core ratio; watch the trend |\n| **over 10%** | VMs are noticeably slowed | Move VMs to a quieter host, cut vCPUs on oversized VMs, or add capacity |\n\nLook at sustained values: short spikes during backups, patching or boot storms are normal.\nToo many vCPUs per physical core raises ready time for every VM on the host, so right-size large VMs first.\n\nVME added this \"CPU Ready-equivalent metric\" in 9.1.0; HPE doesn't document how it's calculated or a threshold. At low load it reads several times higher than the kernel's\nown measurement (*vCPU wait* on the exporter dashboards), so these thresholds err on the side of warning early.\nWhen the two disagree, trust vCPU wait.\n\nThis tile: the most-starved individual vCPU of any VM. One busy vCPU can hide inside a VM's average.",
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "grafana-postgresql-datasource",
            "uid": "vme-tsdb"
          },
          "refId": "A",
          "rawQuery": true,
          "editorMode": "code",
          "format": "table",
          "rawSql": "SELECT max(v) FROM (SELECT last(m.value, m.time) AS v FROM metrics m JOIN servers s ON s.id = m.entity_id WHERE m.time > now() - INTERVAL '15 minutes' AND m.entity_type = 'vm' AND m.metric = 'cpu_ready_max_vcpu_pct' AND (-1 IN ($cluster) OR s.cluster_id IN ($cluster)) AND (-1 IN ($host) OR s.host_id IN ($host)) GROUP BY m.entity_id) x"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "percent",
          "color": {
            "mode": "thresholds"
          },
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "green",
                "value": null
              },
              {
                "color": "#EAB839",
                "value": 5
              },
              {
                "color": "red",
                "value": 10
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "colorMode": "value",
        "graphMode": "none",
        "textMode": "value",
        "justifyMode": "center"
      },
